          "case_name": "DeShaney v. Winnebago County Department of Social Services",
          "citation": "489 U.S. 189 (1989)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1989,
          "date_decided": "1989-02-22",
          "holding": "The Court held the Due Process Clause imposes no affirmative duty on a State to protect a child from violence by a private actor, so caseworkers who knew of the abuse but left the child at home were not liable under § 1983.",
          "relevance": "DeShaney is the federal standard governing whether a child's death or serious injury while known to CPS creates constitutional liability.",
          "quote": "But nothing in the language of the Due Process Clause itself requires the State to protect the life, liberty, and property of its citizens against invasion by private actors.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112202/deshaney-v-winnebago-county-department-of-social-services/",
